0.1.34
- every external program a command runs is recorded twice: in the run's log, as 'Using <program>: <version>.', and in a <program>.version file beside what it made, named for the program in lower case. The results outlive the run -- a genome's tRNAs, proteins and marker tables are carried across from release to release while its sequences are unchanged -- so the version that made them is not the version of the run that last looked at them, and only the file beside them can say. The files are trna/trnascan-se.version (trnascan), prodigal/prodigal.version (prodigal), prodigal/<marker dir>/hmmsearch.version (hmmsearch, Pfam and TIGRFAM alike: PfamScan runs hmmsearch too), busco/busco.version (busco), nhmmer.version and blastn.version in the rna_silva directory and blastn.version in the rna_ltp directory of each genome, gtranslate.version in each trans_table batch, checkm2.version beside ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv (the CheckM2 working directory is removed once the estimates are in that file), checkm.version in each checkm chunk, and makeblastdb.version beside the database generate_ltp_db builds
- a version file is written only where the program made something: a genome tRNAscan-SE or Prodigal refused, a search that failed, a genome skipped because its results were already vouched for -- none is given one, since this run's version would then say something untrue about results it did not make. blastn.version is written in the rna_silva directory only where blastn ran, which is where nhmmer found a gene and there is a database to classify it against
- how each program is asked is one table, utils.common.VERSION_QUERIES, read by program_version(), record_program_version() and write_version_file(). The version is asked once per run where the command starts, not per genome, so a program that will not say what it is is met before a batch is claimed rather than inside one; what it printed is matched against a pattern and never guessed at. The rna_silva nhmmer and blastn lookups that logged 'unknown' where they could not tell are replaced by it
- trnascan no longer hands every genome to its workers with the domain table of the whole release. Both passes gave the pool a bound method, and a bound method is pickled with its instance: 2.7M domain entries, 47 MB and 0.6 s in the parent for each genome, one genome at a time. A batch of 10,000 spent about 1.7 hours 'Checking tRNAs', which is a stat and the hash of a few KB per genome, and the scan after it could feed its workers fewer than two genomes a second however large -c was. The check and the scan are functions of the module now, trnascan_parser() and trnascan_worker(), handed the genome's TrnaJob -- which already carried the domain, settled in the parent for exactly this reason -- and a task is a few hundred bytes. tests/test_trnascan_manager.WhatThePoolIsHanded weighs what each genome sends and fails where a task carries the instance again
- tests/test_common.py is new: 9 tests over how a version is asked for, against programs of a few lines of shell on PATH, including every pattern of the table against what its program really printed on the machine building r237. trnascan, prodigal, hmmsearch and trans_table grow 10 tests over which genomes and batches get a version file and which do not

0.1.33
- a run whose --log cannot be opened is logged to gtdb_migration_tk.log under the command's --out_dir, and says in one line that it was and why. It went to whatever directory the command happened to be run from, which for a command that writes everything else under --out_dir is not where anyone looks. log_candidates() in __main__.py holds the order -- --log, then --out_dir, then the current directory, then the console alone, which cannot fail -- and a release does not end over where its log goes
- logger_setup() clears the handlers of an earlier call before adding its own. Both loggers are named, so a second call is handed the same objects and its handlers were added beside the first call's: every line of the run then reached the console twice. __main__ makes that second call whenever the first could not open the log it was given, which is what -l logs/hmmsearch_pfam.log did where ./logs was a file -- one keystroke from the -l logs of an earlier command, which had created the file
- the bare except that caught it is (OSError, SystemExit), the second because make_sure_path_exists() calls sys.exit() where the directory cannot be made. A bare except swallowed the reason, wrote the run's log somewhere else and printed nothing about either, and the command went on as though it had been given the log it asked for
- tests/test_logger.py is new: 16 tests over what a second logger_setup() does to the console, to the log of the first call and to --silent, that a --log naming a file where a directory is wanted raises so __main__ can fall back, and the order the log is tried in

0.1.32
- prodigal calls a genome in Prodigal's meta mode where single mode refuses it, under the same translation table, as gTranslate does. Single mode trains the model on the genome itself and is the better of the two, so it is tried first and kept where it works; it refuses a draft assembly it cannot train on -- 'saw too many regions of N's' -- however ordinary the bases between the gaps. Eleven genomes of one 2014 submission of N-rich actinomycetes, 9 to 13 Mb each, had been called under neither mode since 2020 and were carried through five releases with an empty protein file; meta mode calls 8,047 to 12,799 genes for each of them. Of the 15 genomes of r237 whose proteome was empty, 12 are called this way and 2 are refused by both modes, being 32% and 43% N
- the genomes that fell back are named in prodigal_meta_fallback.tsv under --out_dir, gathered from each batch's meta_fallback.tsv once every batch has SUCCESS, with what single mode said about each. Their genes were called from Prodigal's precalculated parameters rather than from a model trained on the genome, and the proteome looks like any other afterwards, so a run that did not say which genomes those were would leave nothing downstream able to tell
- each genome that fell back also records it in its own prodigal/prodigal_translation_table.tsv, as a prodigal_mode line beside the table it was called under. A genome called as asked gets no such line, so the file of every other genome of the release is byte for byte what it was
- a Prodigal that refuses a genome without reading it no longer takes the batch down. It closes stdin while the wrapper is still writing the genome in, and the BrokenPipeError that follows is not the RuntimeError the worker catches, so it came out of the pool: the exit status is what says what happened, and a genome neither mode will call is answered for as one genome
- Prodigal runs from the genome's scratch directory. It spools what it is given on stdin into tmp.prodigal.stdin.<pid> in the CURRENT directory and leaves it there when it exits non-zero, so every genome single mode refuses left an uncompressed copy of itself in the working directory of whoever started the run -- 164 MB from twenty of them while this change was being tested. It now goes when the genome's scratch directory does
- tests/test_prodigal_wrapper.py grows 8 tests over the fallback, against a Prodigal of a few lines of shell put on PATH: that single mode is kept where it works, that the retry keeps the translation table, that a genome already in meta mode is not tried again, that a genome neither mode calls raises with what both said, and that a refusal which never reads the genome is not an error of its own. tests/test_prodigal_manager.py grows 7 over what is reported and where

0.1.31
- prodigal calls the genes of a genome whose protein file is empty, rather than counting it as already called. A genome was skipped where its proteins were there and its .sha256 agreed, and an empty file agrees with its digest exactly: prodigal leaves one behind where it failed, and da39a3ee5e6b4b0d3255bfef95601890afd80709 is the SHA-1 of nothing, which is what biolib_lite.checksum.sha256_rb() computes despite its name. GCA_000722275.1 -- 9.9 Mb of Streptomyces in 466 contigs -- has carried an empty proteome since 2020 that way, through r213, r214, r220, r226 and r232, never called again and named as failed nowhere, and it reached hmmsearch as a file with nothing in it. has_proteins() asks what the file holds rather than whether it is there, in prodigal_parser() and again where a run records which genomes it called
- a genome Prodigal will not call costs that genome and not its batch. call_genes() returns a ProdigalFailure rather than raising out of the pool, Prodigal.run() returns what it could not call beside what it could, and those genomes are named in prodigal_not_called.tsv as prodigal_failed with what Prodigal said in the log. Prodigal exits 52 on GCA_000722275.1 saying 'too many regions of N's', and it is the genome and not the run that is wrong: raising took the ten thousand genomes of its batch down with it, and would have done so again on every retry now that an empty proteome is work
- a batch of several genomes in which Prodigal refused every one is still failed, that being Prodigal not working on the machine rather than a batch of difficult genomes; one genome refused on its own is not, since a batch whose last uncalled genome is a bad one would fail identically forever. This is the rule trnascan already follows
- tests/test_prodigal_manager.py grows 13 tests over what an empty proteome is worth and what a refusal costs: that a file agreeing with its digest is still called again when there is nothing in it, that a refused genome is named while the rest of its batch is called, keeps what the last release gave it, and does not fail the batch unless every genome of it was refused

0.1.30
- hmmsearch checks --hmm_db_path before it cuts the release into batches, and stops with one line where it is not the HMMs --db will be searched against. The argument means two different things: PfamScan is handed the DIRECTORY Pfam-A.hmm sits in, hmmsearch is handed the TIGRFAM HMM FILE itself, and neither says so when given the other. Given the directory, hmmsearch reads it as a file that "appears to be empty" and writes no marker table; the r237 TIGRFAM run met that one call later, in a worker, as a FileNotFoundError on the table that was never written. The check is a stat and the five bytes an HMM library begins with, and where a directory is given to --db tigrfam the message names the file inside it that was meant
- a search that failed is raised on where it happened. The TIGRFAM worker ran hmmsearch through os.system(), which returns the exit status to nobody, so the run carried on to read top hits out of a table that did not exist; the status is checked and what hmmsearch said on stderr is what the worker raises. PfamScan checked a stderr it had already merged into stdout, so its check could never fire and a failed search returned its error text to be parsed as hits -- leaving an empty marker table with a checksum vouching for it, which no later run would search again
- a batch whose search processes died is failed rather than finished. search_markers() started the workers, joined them and returned whatever they had done, and BatchCounts.searched is the length of the work list -- what was handed out, not what came back. In r237 every worker of every batch died on its first genome and all 135 batches were written a SUCCESS canary saying searched=10,000, so 303,096 genomes were recorded as searched with no marker table written for any of them and no rerun would look behind those canaries
- a run whose workers died no longer sits there afterwards. The work they never took was left in the queue, and a queue still holding data is joined at exit by a feeder thread waiting for a reader that has gone; the r237 run stayed up for hours after its last batch, with every worker dead, its log finished and nothing left to do
- tests/test_marker_manager.py grows 16 tests over what a search coming to nothing costs: which --hmm_db_path each --db takes and that a wrong one claims no batch, that a worker that raised or exited fails the batch while workers that did the work do not, and that a failed hmmsearch is raised on with what it said rather than met as a missing file

0.1.29
- trnascan takes --gtdb_domain_file and --taxonomy_file and reads the domain of each genome from them, as rna_silva and rna_ltp do, in place of the four NCBI assembly summary files. GTDB's own prediction is preferred, being made from the genome's marker genes rather than from where NCBI filed it, and the standardised NCBI taxonomy answers for the genomes whose Predicted domain is 'None'; the taxonomy is matched on the accession and then on its canonical form, so a GenBank genome finds the lineage recorded against its RefSeq counterpart. A genome neither file answers for is still scanned as a bacterium, which is what this command has always done, but the run says how many of those there were
- the four assembly summary files are gone from this command, and with them the way it decided a domain: nothing in those files says 'bacteria', NCBI telling them apart by directory, so the domain was asserted by which argument each file was passed as and swapping --ga with --gb would have scanned every archaeon as a bacterium in silence. The reader was broken as well as fragile -- a plain open() and one skipped comment line, where a release holds those files gzipped and NCBI writes two comment lines -- so every run against a release died with a UnicodeDecodeError in the constructor before a single genome was scanned
- utils/common.py holds read_taxonomy(), moved out of trans_table.py, which is where a standardised NCBI taxonomy file is now read from. trans_table reads the lineage to decide which genomes a translation table may be doubted for and trnascan reads the domain out of it, and the command modules do not import one another
- trnascan takes --out_dir and cuts the release into batches under it, the same machinery trans_table, prodigal and hmmsearch use (batching.py), so several machines given one --out_dir divide the release between them; --batch_size, --reclaim, --lease and --all behave as they do there, and a batch is skipped once it has SUCCESS. --out_dir holds the state of the run and nothing else: the tRNAs go into each genome's own trna/ directory as they always have, which is why two machines on different batches never write to the same place. Each batch's SUCCESS canary carries what it came to and the release totals are added up from them
- a genome that got no tRNAs is named in trnascan_not_scanned.tsv with the reason -- no_genomic_fasta where its sequences are not where the release says they are, trnascan_failed where tRNAscan-SE returned an error on it -- rather than being dropped in silence, and neither costs the other ten thousand genomes of the batch. A batch in which several genomes were to be scanned and every one of them failed is failed instead, that being tRNAscan-SE not working on the machine rather than a batch of difficult genomes; one genome failing on its own is not, since a batch whose last unscanned genome is a bad one would otherwise fail identically on every retry
- --all no longer takes the run down on a genome whose genomic FASTA is not there. It marked every genome of the release for scanning without looking, so the first missing file reached gzip.open() in a worker and the exception came out through the pool; the batch's genomes are now checked by batching.split_by_fasta(), which is what prodigal and hmmsearch check with
- rerunning trnascan over a release with nothing left to do no longer raises ZeroDivisionError. The progress bar was given smoothing=50/len(genome_files), and a release whose genomes all have valid tRNAs leaves that list empty
- trnascan honours --tmp_dir, and decompresses each genome into it rather than into whatever tempfile.mkdtemp() picks, which is /tmp: on a machine running -c 40 that was forty decompressed genomes in /tmp at once. The directory is made at startup, so a --tmp_dir that cannot be made is met once rather than failing every batch this machine takes
- a genome in none of the four assembly summary files is still scanned as a bacterium, which is what this command has always done, but the run now says how many of those there were instead of leaving an archaeon quietly scanned with the bacterial model
- the dead code in trnascan_manager.py is gone: __workerThread(), a byte-for-byte duplicate of the worker that nothing called; the commented-out report parse, which was the third copy of a parse that now has one reader and which this command never needed, the checksum beside a genome's own results being what decides its work; the unused genomes_to_consider; the commented-out process pool at the end of run(); and the CPU count printed to stdout rather than logged. The module carries type hints and a docstring saying why it is shaped as it is
- tests/test_trnascan_manager.py is new: 25 tests with subprocess.Popen stubbed, over the domain each genome is scanned with, what decides whether a genome is scanned at all, the two ways a genome can come out with no tRNAs, and the batching

0.1.28
- the metadata command is called genomic_metadata. It generates each genome's own nucleotide and gene metadata, while the ten tables the database is loaded from are built by create_tables, and one name covering the per genome half of a step whose other half is a different command said neither. The old name is not kept: argparse refuses it with the list of commands, so a script written against it stops rather than doing something else
- the genomic_metadata command no longer ends the release on one genome whose files are not there. A genome is checked for its genomic FASTA and for the GFF prodigal wrote, and where either is absent it is warned about and named in metadata_missing_files.tsv, one row per missing file, with the path that was looked for. The check was check_file_exists(), which logs and calls sys.exit(), and it ran inside a pool worker once per genome: the first straggler of a release generated while the gene calling of its last genomes was still finishing took the run down and threw away however many of the million were already done. A warning alone would not do either, being one line in a log of a million, so the list is a file
- how much of a genome is done follows from which of its files are there. The nucleotide statistics need only the FASTA and are written whenever the FASTA is, so a genome whose genes are not called yet still gets its metadata.genome_nt.tsv; create_tables reads the two tables independently, so that half is a row rather than a broken genome, and it is not calculated again when prodigal catches up. The gene statistics are a coding density, needing the genome size as well as the GFF, so a genome with no sequences yields neither file and its directory is left as it was found, old genometk.log included
- genomic_metadata therefore takes --out_dir, required, which holds that report and nothing else; the metadata goes into the genome directories as it always has. The report is written whether or not anything was missing, as trans_table's gtranslate_no_prediction.tsv is, so that a release with nothing missing says so rather than leaving the reader to wonder how far the run got. The warnings are logged by the parent process as the results come back rather than by the workers, several processes appending to one log file interleaving as they do
- tests/test_metadata_manager.py is new: seventeen tests over real genomes, a few hundred bases of FASTA and a GFF with one CDS, so the calculators run rather than being stubbed. What they hold to is that one genome short of a file costs that genome and not the release, which is what the exit did
- biolib_lite.common.get_num_lines() closes the file and the mmap it opens. It closed neither, so every caller leaked a descriptor and a mapping per call
- metadata_manager.py carries type hints, as marker_manager.py and prodigal.py already do: every method, its parameters and both classes' instance attributes, with the one line docstrings filled out to say what each file the method reads is and what it leaves behind. The two classes there do the two halves of the metadata step -- MetadataManager calculates each genome's nucleotide and gene statistics into its own directory, MetadataTable gathers those and the rRNA and tRNA results of the whole release into the ten tables the database is loaded from -- and nothing in the signatures said so
- MetadataTable.create_metadata_tables_multi() and process_genome_worker() are gone. They were an unfinished multiprocessing copy of create_metadata_tables() that could not run: the worker read self.silva_output_dir, self.ltp_output_dir and a gpath it never unpacked, none of which exist on MetadataTable, having been copied from rna_manager_ltp.py; the caller read a self.cpus MetadataTable does not have and a numlines it never bound, passed the worker two values where it unpacked three, and looped a second time over a file handle its first loop had exhausted. Nothing called either, and main.py dispatches create_tables to create_metadata_tables() alone. Annotating them would have meant writing types for code no interpreter has ever reached
- the five files checked in with CRLF line endings are LF, like the other eighty. metadata_manager.py and strains.py held both, so a single change anywhere in either could rewrite the whole file in the diff and bury itself. Nothing but the endings changed in seq_tk.py, database_configuration/__init__.py, lpsn.py and strains.py
- every argparse help string and subcommand description in __main__.py ends with a period, as does every line of the command table in print_help(). Fifty-eight of them did not, and of the thirty-six lines of the command table exactly one did, so the punctuation looked as though it distinguished something. Only the punctuation changed; no help text was reworded

0.1.27
- hmmsearch takes an --out_dir and cuts the release into batches under it, the same machinery trans_table and prodigal use (batching.py), so the marker search can be spread over several machines. Several machines given the same --out_dir divide the release between them, each claiming batches no other holds; --batch_size, --reclaim, --lease and --all behave as they do for prodigal, and a batch is skipped once it has SUCCESS. --out_dir holds the state of the run and nothing else: the marker tables go into each genome's prodigal/ directory as they always have, which is why two machines on different batches never write to the same place
- the batches of one run live under <out_dir>/<marker dir>/, e.g. <out_dir>/pfam_33.1_lite/batch_000001/. One run searches one database at one version, and a run of the other database over the same release is different work for the same genomes: sharing batch directories would have the SUCCESS canary of a Pfam batch tell a TIGRFAM run that batch was done. One --out_dir therefore carries both, and annotating against a new Pfam release gets its own directory beside them for the same reason
- batching.plan_batches() takes the file a batch is planned around rather than assuming the genomic FASTA: genome_file(accession, genome_dir), defaulting to the new genome_fasta_of() and given utils.common.protein_fasta by hmmsearch, which reads the proteins prodigal called. It is the batchfile's first column and what split_by_fasta() stats, so a batch never names files its command will not open. protein_fasta() is new in utils/common.py, that path having been assembled from its three pieces in five places
- a genome whose proteins are missing or are an empty file is named in the batch's not_searched.tsv under no_protein_file and left, rather than stopping the other ten thousand genomes of the batch; hmmsearch_not_searched.tsv gathers them for the release once every batch has SUCCESS, as prodigal_not_called.tsv does. Each batch's SUCCESS canary carries what it came to (searched, already_searched, not_searched) and the release totals are added up from them, a machine that ran the last batch having searched none of the others
- the marker search no longer swallows every exception. It caught the lot and returned, which under batching would have a batch that searched nothing be written a SUCCESS canary no machine ever looks behind; a failure now fails the batch, which is retried by a later run, and a KeyboardInterrupt gives the claim up rather than leaving the batch to sit out its lease. writeProc.terminate was also missing its parentheses and never ran
- --db is checked where it is read rather than left to fail later: an unrecognised value raised NameError on a variable the branch never bound. argparse limits it to pfam and tigrfam, so this is reachable only by a caller going around it
- hmmsearch honours --tmp_dir. Both workers decompress each genome's proteins before searching them and called tempfile.mkdtemp() with no dir, so every copy went to /tmp whatever --tmp_dir said: on a machine running -c 40 that is forty decompressed proteomes in /tmp at once, and a scratch directory chosen to keep them off it did nothing. The directory is also made at startup rather than by the first worker that wants it, so a --tmp_dir that cannot be made is met once, before any batch is claimed, instead of failing every batch this machine takes
- the Pfam worker no longer prints the path of each scratch copy to stdout. It was a debug print from the original rewrite, one line per genome searched, and the TIGRFAM worker had no equivalent, so a Pfam run was noisy and a TIGRFAM run was not
- hmmsearch says truthfully whether the report and the disk agree about a genome whose marker table cannot be vouched for. That warning asserted the genome 'was not marked for reannotation' without looking, which is false for exactly the genome it most often describes: one the release DOES call new, whose annotation run was interrupted partway through writing the table. It is now guarded, saying either 'and is marked as new or modified' or 'though it is not marked for reannotation'. The other two cross-check warnings are unchanged, including the one for a genome annotated already though the release calls it new -- expected of a batch being resumed, and worth saying every time it happens

0.1.26
- marker_manager.py and biolib_lite/external/pfam_search.py carry type hints, as prodigal.py already did: every method and the instance attributes, with the shapes that a bare tuple or dict cannot explain named at module level. MarkerJob is one genome as marker_parser() receives it; PfamTopHits and TigrTopHits are the two top hit tables, which differ because a gene keeps its best Pfam hit for each family it matched and one TIGRFAM hit overall -- the same function name over two shapes, which is worth stating rather than inferring from the indexing
- hmmsearch skips a genome with one value rather than two, which stops it losing workers to its own work list. marker_parser() returned 'null' for a genome already annotated but fell off the end as None for one whose _protein.faa.gz is missing or zero bytes, and run_hmmsearch() filtered only 'null'. The None went onto workerQueue, which ends with one None per worker as the signal to stop, and no worker can tell the two apart: whichever drew it exited with the rest of the release still queued, silently, and with enough such genomes every worker could stop before searching anything. Both cases are None now and the filter drops None, so a skipped genome is left out of the queue rather than put on it. The progress denominator counts what will actually be searched, where it used to include the skipped genomes and never reach 100%
- tests/test_marker_manager.py covers this, driving the real run_hmmsearch() with the multiprocessing stubbed out so that what reaches the work queue can be read back: the queue ends with one None per worker and holds none anywhere else, whether a genome is skipped for being annotated already, for having no protein file, or for having an empty one. The empty case means zero bytes on disk, which is what the code stats -- a gzip of nothing is still twenty-odd bytes of header
- _tigr_top_hit() builds its table with {} rather than defaultdict(dict). Every value it stores is a tuple, so the factory contradicted the table's own shape, and every read is guarded by 'if gene_id in tophits', so it was never reached. PfamSearch.run() no longer wraps its body in a try/except that re-raised the same exception unchanged

0.1.25
- hmmsearch and checkm can read the report of a release again. Both took --report and parsed it themselves, expecting the three column '<DOMAIN>\t<accession>\t<attr;attr>' row that update_refseq wrote until 0.0.9, when the domain column went because nothing fed it any more. Against the two column report.log written since, hmmsearch raised IndexError on the first line; checkm raised it too, and had a second problem behind it: 'modified' became four comparison outcomes in 0.1.7, so even reindexed, a parser looking for 'new' or 'modified' would have annotated the new genomes and silently skipped every genome whose FASTA changed -- a release that looks complete and is not
- the report now has one reader, report_accessions() in update_genomes.py, which owns both ends of the file with the STATUS_ constants beside it. Two questions are built on it: genomes_to_regenerate() gives the genomes the release did not bring derived data with (STATUS_REGENERATE: 'new' and 'genomic FASTA file changed', the direct translation of the old 'new' and 'modified'), and genomes_in_release() gives every genome the release holds a directory for (STATUS_IN_RELEASE, adding the two carried-across outcomes). hmmsearch and checkm call these instead of splitting rows; nothing outside update_genomes.py parses a report row. A test asserts every STATUS_ constant is in one set or deliberately in neither, so the next outcome added is a decision rather than the commands quietly disagreeing
- checkm's --all_genomes means every genome the release HOLDS rather than every row of the report that is not 'removed'. Under the new vocabulary the old reading swept in the to_curate genomes, which were never copied into the release -- both MD5s are read before the copytree, so a comparison that raised leaves nothing behind -- and looking one up in the genome_dirs file raised KeyError. Without a report ('none') it now considers every genome the genome path file names, where it raised NameError
- a report written before 0.0.9 is refused with a message naming the line, rather than read as though it were current. Its first column is a domain name, so no row matches any outcome and the command would annotate nothing while reporting success
- hmmsearch and top_hit take --dir_suffix rather than --folder_suffix, and its help gives the default it resolves to for each --db instead of pointing at config.py. config.MARKER_FOLDER_SUFFIX is renamed MARKER_DIR_SUFFIX to match

0.1.24
- The tests of the shared batching machinery move from tests/test_trans_table.py to tests/test_batching.py, which is where batching.py's contracts belong now that it is neither command's. They run under a BatchLayout naming no real command: the mechanism is told which files a batch keeps, and a test that passed only under trans_table's filenames would be testing trans_table rather than the batching. tests/test_ncbi_utils.py takes the genomic_fasta tests, that function having moved there

0.1.23
- The Prodigal wrapper builds a genome's results beside their destination and moves them onto it, rather than writing into it. os.replace() is atomic within a filesystem, so a reader -- the next run deciding whether the genome still needs calling, or the command that takes the release -- sees either the whole file or the one that was there before, and never a half-written one. These files are the release itself and not scratch: a half-written one beside a genome is swept up by nothing
- Two writers can meet on one genome. prodigal cuts a release into batches of disjoint genomes, so it takes two machines holding the SAME batch, which --reclaim does deliberately and an expired lease does to a machine that has stalled rather than stopped. This does not make that safe and is not meant to -- the proteins of one and the .sha256 of the other still pair up wrongly -- but the mismatch is then DETECTED, the digest not matching the file, and the genome is called again by the next run, where before a corrupt gzip was carried into the release

0.1.22
- The second progress bar of a prodigal batch says "Checking called genes" rather than "Checking genomes", which the first one says. They ask different questions -- the first stats the genomic FASTA going in, on threads, to find the genomes the release does not hold; the second reads the called genes already sitting there, on the worker processes, to find the genomes that need no calling -- and two bars a batch under one label left a reader unable to tell which pass they were watching

0.1.21
- prodigal takes an --out_dir and cuts the release into batches under it, so that several machines can be pointed at one directory and divide the release between them as they already do for trans_table. --batch_size, --reclaim and --lease behave as they do there, a batch is claimed before it is worked on and skipped once it has SUCCESS, what happens to a batch is written in the batch, and a batch that fails is retried by a later run rather than costing the release. --out_dir is now required
- What --out_dir holds is the state of the run and nothing else. The called genes go into each genome's own prodigal/ directory, as they always have, which is also why two machines working on different batches never write to the same place: the batches are disjoint sets of genomes and the results follow the genome, not the batch
- prodigal_not_called.tsv at the top of --out_dir names every genome of the release that got no genes and why -- no_translation_table, no_genomic_fasta or prodigal_failed. Three different failures, told apart because the next command needs to know which genomes have no proteins and should not have to look in 135 directories to find out
- A rerun skips finished batches rather than re-reading the proteins of the whole release to find out they are still there, which is what batching buys over the per-genome checksum alone. The checksum still decides genome by genome inside a batch that is not finished, which is what a batch retried after a failure leans on. --all_genomes does the finished batches again, since otherwise it would skip every batch it was asked to redo
- batching.py holds the machinery both commands share: cutting a release into batches, the batchfiles, the canaries, claims as leases, the heartbeat, the per-batch log, and writing and joining a headered TSV. It was trans_table's and is now neither command's; what differs between them is only what a batch is FOR, and what differs in name travels in a BatchLayout. Nothing in it knows about gTranslate or Prodigal. genomic_fasta() moves to ncbi_utils, where what more than one command knows about NCBI's files belongs

0.1.20
- prodigal leaves a genome with no translation table uncalled rather than refusing the release. gTranslate returns no prediction for a handful of genomes -- eight of r237's 1,346,118, two of which have no genomic FASTA to predict from at all -- and there is nothing to call their genes under: letting Prodigal pick a table by coding density is the very thing handing it the summary is there to prevent. Until now one such genome stopped the whole run, so a release could not have its genes called until every gap had been filled by hand with --tt_override. They are now named in the log, with a pointer to trans_table's gtranslate_no_prediction.tsv which lists them in full, and --tt_override remains how one is given a table and called after all
- Which genomes those are is still settled before any genes are called rather than discovered one at a time, because a run that meets the gap genome by genome meets it hours in, having already called everything ahead of it
- A summary covering NO genome of the release still stops the run. That is the wrong --trans_table file, or a release trans_table has not been run over, rather than a release with a few unpredictable genomes; carrying on would call nothing at all and report that the run had finished

0.1.19
- trans_table writes gtranslate_no_prediction.tsv, the genomes of the release it has no translation table for and why. Every batch has always named its own in no_prediction.tsv as it was predicted, and that was the only place they were reported: a release finished across five machines over days left them in 135 directories to be found by whoever thought to look. prodigal needs a table for every genome of the release, and this is the list to correct with --tt_override. The file is written even when it is empty, so that a release with nothing missing says so rather than leaving the question open
