- The list is worked out per batch from what the batch was ASKED about against what its summary answered, rather than concatenated from the batches' own no_prediction.tsv files. Those are written by the run that predicts a batch, and a batch already predicted is never predicted again, so a release resumed or finished across several machines can hold batches that never wrote one; the batchfile and the summary, which every batch certainly has, cannot miss a genome that way

0.1.18
- A batch's plan is written as gtranslate_batchfile.tsv.gz. gTranslate cannot read it -- it opens a batchfile with a plain open() -- so the copy handed to gTranslate, gtranslate_batchfile_present.tsv, is now always written rather than only where a genome's FASTA was missing, and is removed once gTranslate has finished with it. A finished batch therefore keeps the compressed plan alone, while the uncompressed copy exists only while the batch is being worked on. A batch taken over before gTranslate finished writes that copy again from the plan, which is why removing it costs nothing; a batch gTranslate failed on keeps it, being what a retry looks at to see what went in
- A batch planned by an earlier version holds gtranslate_batchfile.tsv uncompressed, and batchfile_path() reads that where it is there. A finished output directory is still read -- the command is run again over one to pick up the work since added to it -- and a release whose batches looked unplanned would be partitioned again with its batches already done, moving genomes between batches that have finished

0.1.17
- trans_table writes gtranslate_ncbi_tt_comparison.tsv.gz, every genome gTranslate and NCBI BOTH called a table for, agreements and all: genome_id, gtranslate_tt, ncbi_tt, checkm_tt, ncbi_conflict, checkm_conflict, coding_density_4, coding_density_11, gc_percent, n50, genome_size and ncbi_taxonomy. ncbi_conflict is the plain inequality and is what decides whether a genome is also in ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv; checkm_conflict beside it is the narrower question of whether the density rule and gTranslate disagree about the genome being recoded at all. The agreements are there because the rate the two differ at, and whether the genomes they differ about are unlike the ones they agree about, cannot be asked of a file holding only the disagreements. The conflicts remain a file of their own, being the few hundred rows of a release worth reading and not something to be filtered out of a million first. A genome NCBI has not annotated is in neither, having nothing to compare against and no ncbi_conflict to report
- The conflicts are the comparison FILTERED rather than a second walk over the genomes, by conflicts_from_comparison(), which takes the conflict file's columns from the comparison row by name. Every GFF of a release has already been read once to make the comparison, and the two files can no longer come to disagree about which genomes conflicted
- ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv and the comparison both carry gc_percent, n50 and genome_size as gTranslate measured them, taken from its summary rather than measured again -- reading 1.3M genomes a second time to learn what has already been learnt. They are what tells a conflict that is a real disagreement about a real genome from one about a few hundred kb of something barely assembled
- The comparison is gzipped in the batch as well as in the release, being a row per genome compared -- 786,144 of r237's 1.35M -- while the conflicts stay plain text, being meant to be opened and read. concatenate() reads a batch file gzipped or not, so the release file of each is joined from whatever its batches hold

0.1.16
- trans_table writes the release's gtranslate.translation_table_summary.tsv.gz gzipped. It is a row per genome of the release -- 116 MB of text for r237 against 34 MB compressed -- and is the one file of the output directory whose size is the size of the release. ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv is not compressed: it is a few hundred rows meant to be looked at. A batch's own summary is left alone, being gTranslate's output, written by gTranslate into the batch directory and not this command's to name or to compress
- read_translation_table_summary() decides whether a summary is gzipped from the file's first two bytes rather than from its name, so prodigal takes a batch's summary and a release's alike, and a release summary that has been gunzipped, or renamed on the way to another machine, still reads. That is the single reader both commands share, so neither can come to disagree with the other about it
- trans_table removes an uncompressed release summary an earlier version left beside the compressed one. Two files a genome apart of which one is stale, both named for the run, is how a release gets its genes called under the wrong tables

0.1.15
- trans_table removes the checkm2 directory once the CheckM2 estimates are in ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv. What it held -- the staged genomes, the called proteins, the DIAMOND output -- is about 600 KB per genome per table, a few hundred megabytes for a release, and says nothing the conflict file does not now say. It is removed last of all, after the annotated file is written, so that nothing is swept up that has not landed. What this gives up is a later run reading the reports rather than making them again, which is minutes for the few hundred genomes a release conflicts about, and is not what the resumability is for: the batches are days of prediction and are never removed. A table CheckM2 produced nothing for keeps the directory instead, so that retrying that table does not also redo the tables that worked

0.1.14
- ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv says beside each pair of CheckM2 numbers whether they pass standard GTDB QC, as pass_qc_gtranslate_tt and pass_qc_ncbi_tt: completeness above 50%, contamination below 10%, and a quality score of completeness less five times contamination above 50. All three have to hold and they are not one condition said three ways -- the score alone would keep a genome 96% complete carrying 9% contamination, and the completeness alone would keep anything that had been sequenced at all. The thresholds are exclusive as GTDB states them, so a genome exactly 50% complete or at exactly 10% contamination does not pass. The verdict is what the release actually keeps or drops a genome by, so a conflict that changes it is a different thing from one that moves the numbers a little, and it is given per table because a genome can pass under the table gTranslate predicted and fail under the one NCBI declares -- which is the case worth looking at. A genome CheckM2 returned nothing for is na and not False: it was not looked at and found wanting. Each verdict sits beside the numbers it was reached from rather than at the end of the row, so that a row read by eye is two answers to one question and not four numbers followed by two verdicts. How many of the conflicting genomes pass under each table is logged

0.1.13
- ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv carries what CheckM2 makes of each conflicting genome under EACH of the two tables in dispute, as cm2_completeness_gtranslate_tt, cm2_contamination_gtranslate_tt, cm2_completeness_ncbi_tt and cm2_contamination_ncbi_tt. A conflict is a genome two callers disagree about and the question it raises is which of them is right; completeness is the evidence, since genes called under the wrong code are truncated at every TGA and the markers CheckM2 counts go with them. One run would say how good the genome is, two say which table makes it look like a genome at all. CheckM2's own choice is not asked for: left to itself it picks between tables 4 and 11 by coding density, which is what checkm_tt already reports and which cannot express 25. The runs are made once for the release and grouped by table, not once per batch -- the conflicts are a few hundred genomes of a million-odd, two or three per batch, and CheckM2 loads its models and searches the whole DIAMOND database once per run whatever the run holds, so grouped by table the whole release is one run per table in dispute. A release already predicted picks this up by running the command again: every batch is SUCCESS and is skipped, and the work happens where the release files are written. A run whose quality_report.tsv is already there is read rather than made again. CheckM2 is reached as an external program on PATH, as gTranslate is, and is checked for before a run starts; each genome is linked as <accession>.fna.gz first, because CheckM2 names a result for the file it read and NCBI names the file for the assembly. A genome with no FASTA, a run that fails, or a table CheckM2 returns nothing for leaves na in those four columns and the rest of the row intact: the conflicts are found, written and counted before CheckM2 starts, and nothing about estimating their quality can cost a release days of prediction
- trans_table reports how many genomes NCBI declares a translation table for and what percentage of THOSE gTranslate disagrees with, per batch as before and now for the release as well. The denominator is the genomes that could be compared and not the genomes of the release: a genome NCBI has not annotated is not one the two agree or disagree about, and counting it in would turn the number into a measure of how much of the release NCBI has annotated. The release figure is added up from the batches' SUCCESS canaries rather than recomputed, because a release is predicted by several machines and the machine running the last batch has compared none of the others. Each batch now records the conflicts it found and the genomes it could not compare there alongside the genomes it compared; a release predicted before those fields existed still reports the count and the rate, and says nothing about the genomes NCBI declares no table for rather than reporting a total short by every batch finished then

0.1.12
- ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv says beside checkm_tt whether the coding density rule and gTranslate disagree about the genome being RECODED, which is the disagreement that matters: 11 is the standard code and 4 and 25 are recodings of it, and a genome whose genes are called under the wrong kind has them truncated or run together at every TGA. checkm_conflict is True where gTranslate says 11 and the rule says 4, and where gTranslate says 4 or 25 and the rule says 11. It is False for 25 against 4: the rule calls genes under tables 4 and 11 and picks between those two alone, so 4 is the only recoding it can ever return and is as close as it can come to saying 25 -- which is what gTranslate is run for
- trans_table writes ncbi_tt_conflict.tsv in place of ncbi_tt_comparison.tsv, holding only the genomes gTranslate and NCBI disagree about. The comparison is asked for the disagreements -- a genome whose genes GTDB would call under a table NCBI rejects, and whether such genomes fall together in the taxonomy -- and agreement is nearly every genome of a release, so the file was 1.3M rows of which the interesting ones were a handful and every row of it had to be filtered before it could be read. The result column goes with them: every row is now a conflict, so it said 'conflict' and nothing else. Nothing consumed either file; prodigal takes the translation table summary, which is unchanged. How many genomes agreed, conflicted, and have no table from NCBI to compare is logged per batch as before, that line now being the only place the agreements are counted
- trans_table ends with an error in the log when a batch has failed, rather than raising. The batch that failed has already said why in its own log and in its FAILED file, and the command's last line named the directories to look in and said that running it again retries them -- all of which was then buried under 'Unexpected error' and a traceback out of the argparse frames, on a run of five machines over days in which every batch but two had succeeded and stayed succeeded. run() returns False where a batch failed, True where every batch this machine took finished; the exit code is unchanged
0.1.11
- a trans_table claim on a batch is a lease rather than a note of who took it. The machine holding a batch touches its RUNNING file every five minutes and a claim untouched for --lease hours is taken by whichever machine next comes to the batch, wherever it was made. What it replaces asked whether the PID a claim named was running, which answers the question only on the machine that made the claim and answers it wrongly there: PIDs are reused, so after a reset the PID is as likely to belong to something new as to be missing, and the batch is then skipped as busy by every machine forever. A machine that is running but wedged -- hung on a mount that went away, which is what a reset host leaves the machines around it -- reads as busy to a liveness check and as gone to a lease, and gone is what it is. The age of a claim is measured against the clock of the file server holding it, taken from a file created in the same directory, so two machines need not agree about the time; --reclaim remains, for taking a claim before its lease is up
- trans_table records PREDICTED when gTranslate returns and compares rather than predicts a batch that carries it. The prediction is the hours of a batch and the comparison that follows is seconds, so a machine lost between the two, or a comparison that failed on a malformed GFF, cost a batch of 10,000 genomes its whole gene calling on the retry. Within a batch gTranslate already resumes by itself -- it writes each genome's called genes with a checksum beside them, skips a genome whose files verify, and clears the intermediates only when it exits cleanly -- so a batch interrupted at genome 7,000 of 10,000 carries on from genome 7,000, which is why nothing here removes a batch directory before retrying it
- trans_table passes gTranslate --force unless --no_force says not to, and names the genomes it dropped. gTranslate calls genes in worker processes and ends the whole run when one dies, so a single genome it cannot handle takes the other 9,999 with it and does so identically on every retry: of the r237 batches, one was lost to a 255 bp assembly with no genes, whose glycine count of zero divides into the codon density, and another to a 4.75 Mb assembly Prodigal refuses for its runs of N. A genome dropped that way is simply absent from the summary, so the accessions gTranslate returned no prediction for are written to no_prediction.tsv in the batch directory and warned about, rather than being discovered later by prodigal refusing to call a release for want of a table
- what happens to a trans_table batch is written to trans_table.log in the batch's own directory. Every machine of a run writes its own --log, and five machines appending to one log file over NFS do not interleave: they overwrite one another at their own cached offsets. The r237 log was 82,035 bytes of which 33,276 were NUL padding, holding no trace of one of the five machines, of the batch it was working on, or of a single one of the failures. A batch directory is written to by the one machine holding the batch, so what it says survives
- a trans_table batch that failed keeps what it said as FAILED.<timestamp> when it is retried. Claiming a batch has to clear FAILED or the batch would read as failed while it runs, and deleting it took with it the only record of why -- which on a batch that fails the same way every time is the thing to read. A run interrupted with Ctrl-C now gives its claim back rather than leaving the batch to sit out its lease
0.1.9
- trans_table asks whether a genome's FASTA is on disk as each batch is run, rather than of the whole release before any batch exists. Planning stat-ed all 1.35M genomes of r237 over NFS at 12-23 ms each -- 4 to 8 hours between 'Read N genomes' and the next line logged, with no progress bar, and nothing written until it ended, so a run stopped in that step had no plan to resume from and began again. The check cannot simply go: gTranslate checks the paths of a batchfile itself and refuses the WHOLE batch if one is missing, which for a batch of 10,000 would fail identically on every retry. Asked of a batch it is ~1 minute against the hours gTranslate then runs, it happens while other machines work, and a machine lost during it costs one batch; the plan is now cut from the genome_dirs file alone and written in under 4 seconds, which also leaves which genomes share a batch following from that file rather than from what stat said the day the plan was cut. A genome left out is named in missing_genomic_fasta.tsv in its batch directory and gTranslate is handed gtranslate_batchfile_present.tsv, both written only when something is missing; the batchfile the plan cut stays the record of which genomes the batch is. The stats are made 32 at a time (3-7 ms a genome, the file server rather than the threads being the limit), one os.stat replacing os.path.exists() and os.path.getsize(), which asked the server the same question twice
- update_genomes keeps the derived data of a genome whose previous release holds a md5checksums.txt that cannot name its genomic FASTA. NCBI served, for a time, a manifest listing its own scratch files and no genome file at all, and release 232 holds it for eight genomes; every one of them failed the comparison outright and carried nothing into the new release, though the FASTA beside it is intact and byte for byte the mirror's. The manifest is only ever a way of not hashing the file, so where it cannot answer the file is hashed instead and the comparison goes on as usual -- all eight are unchanged. A previous release that can produce no readable FASTA either has its genome built from the mirror alone, as a new genome is, and both cases are noted in to_review.log, the previous release holding something that wants correcting whichever way the genome went. A previous genome directory that is not there at all still fails, being an --old_genome_dirs_file naming a tree that has moved rather than one genome's bad manifest, and the mirror's own manifest not naming the FASTA still fails too, that being the sync's to answer for
- update_genomes --resume continues a run that was interrupted, doing again only what the genome_dirs.tsv it left behind does not name. That file already recorded exactly what a stopped run had placed -- a genome is written to it once its directory is there -- so nothing new has to be tracked to know where a run got to. Without it an interrupted run had to be repeated in full: on a release of 1.35M genomes, 38-47 hours of recopying to redo the 87% that was already on disk, and the first genome of the add pass would have failed on copytree rather than overwrite what was there. The reports are rewritten rather than appended to, carrying the rows of the genomes the earlier run finished and dropping those of the genomes it did not, so the release is described once through and a genome that failed for want of disk is described by what happens to it this time. Directories a run was in the middle of writing are replaced, since they are not in the file and so are placed again
- prodigal writes each genome's results where they belong instead of into a scratch directory for the parent to move out of. Every output file was written three times -- uncompressed by Prodigal, gzipped into --tmp_dir, then copied to the genome's directory, which is NFS where --tmp_dir is local, so the move was always a copy -- and the protein file was then read back off NFS, decompressed and hashed by the parent, one genome at a time after the pool had finished. The worker now gzips straight to the paths the task names and takes the checksum from the bytes already passing through it, so the copy and the whole serial hashing pass are gone. The paths are given to the wrapper rather than composed by it: what the files are called is GTDB's convention, not a general Prodigal wrapper's, and the wrapper no longer works out any genome's accession -- the caller knows it and puts it in the task. What is left in prodigal_manager.py after the gene calling is the one record the wrapper has no business knowing about, prodigal_translation_table.tsv
- the scratch directory of a worker is checked to contain none of the results before anything is written, because everything in it is deleted when the genome is done. tempfile.mkdtemp() makes a fresh uniquely named directory so it cannot contain them, but the results are now the release's own gene calls rather than files in /tmp, and a scratch directory that turned out to be an ancestor of one would take them with it. --tmp_dir is now where that scratch directory is made rather than where the results are staged
- the digest prodigal writes beside each protein file is computed with SHA-1, which is what biolib_lite.checksum.sha256_rb() has always computed despite its name. It is the function that reads these digests back to decide a genome can be skipped, and every .sha256 file of every past release holds a SHA-1: computing a real SHA-256 would match none of them and would have the next run call the genes of the whole release again. The file is written without a trailing newline, as before
- the accession at the front of an NCBI assembly name is ncbi_utils.assembly_accession(). The rule -- the accession ends at the second underscore, GCF_000157115.2_Escherichia_sp_3_2_53FAA_V2 being GCF_000157115.2 -- was written out in nine places, two of which now have to agree with each other: the wrapper looked up a genome's table with it and prodigal_manager named that genome's files with it. It is not canonical_gid(), which gives G000157115
- the coding base masks count the last base of the rightmost gene on every contig. A GFF counts bases from 1 and the mask was allocated one entry per base, so the index of that gene's end lay one past the end of the array and numpy dropped the assignment without a word: one base lost per contig, which for a genome of a few hundred contigs is a coding density low by a few hundredths of a percent. It is small either way, and it fed the choice between tables 4 and 11 in prodigal and the coding_density of the GTDB metadata table
- the two other parsers of this shape, in metadata_ncbi_manager.py and genometk_lite/metadata_genes.py, keep the genes of a contig as a list of intervals as well. They are copies of the same code and carried the same two faults: gene IDs from a counter reset only when a contig was first met, which overwrote genes when a GFF returned to an earlier contig, and the mask short by one. They read NCBI's GFFs rather than Prodigal's, where nothing here controls whether a contig's features arrive together, and what they measure is the coding density published in the metadata
- ProdigalGeneFeatureParser keeps the genes of a contig as a list of intervals rather than under gene IDs of its own making. The counter those IDs came from was one variable, reset whenever a contig was met for the FIRST time, so a GFF that returned to an earlier contig reused IDs that contig already had and overwrote its own genes: three CDS lines for a contig came back as two, and the coding density with them. Prodigal writes each contig's genes together so it never fired here, and nothing read the IDs; it was a trap for the next GFF this parser is pointed at. The '# Model Data' line is no longer parsed for a translation table: it was read by splitting on ';' and taking field four, a position that is Prodigal's business and that raises IndexError on a header with fewer fields, and nothing in the package ever read what it set. The file is read under a with statement
- the Prodigal wrapper hands each worker its own genome's translation table instead of every genome's. The settings a run was given were put on the instance and read back inside the worker, which cost nothing while the workers were forked; a pool pickles the callable once per task, so the whole mapping went down the pipe for each genome -- measured at 10 MB and about 0.1s per genome for a release of half a million, some 14 hours and 5 TB of pipe traffic before Prodigal had called a gene. The worker is now a module-level function taking a ProdigalTask of one genome, its table, and where the results go: 217 bytes. Which table a genome uses is resolved in the parent by table_of(), the worker having no business holding the mapping to answer for one genome
- the Prodigal wrapper no longer writes an uncompressed copy of every genome to disk to feed Prodigal, nor measures coding densities that decide nothing. The genome is piped to Prodigal's stdin, which it reads when given no -i, and the genes called are byte identical; the copy was written once per translation table and read straight back by the next process. The densities exist to choose between tables 4 and 11, so they are measured only when there is a choice to make: with the table known, one table is run and parsing its GFF to build a mask nothing reads was 0.035s and a 41 MB allocation per genome. The mask, when it is built, is bool rather than the float64 numpy gives by default -- 5 MB for a 5 Mb genome rather than 41 MB, in every worker at once. The three outputs are gzipped straight into the output directory instead of being gzipped in the temporary directory and copied out, which read and wrote each of them twice. Prodigal runs through subprocess with its exit code checked and its stderr kept, rather than through os.system with stderr sent to /dev/null and the status never looked at, which turned a genome Prodigal failed on into an empty file noticed much later by whatever went to read it. The genome's size comes from seq_tk.genome_size(), which streams the sequences rather than holding every contig of it in memory. Proteins and nucleotide genes are unchanged byte for byte; the GFF now carries the full contig description in its header, which writing the genome out through write_fasta() had been discarding
- the vendored biolib Parallel class is gone, and the four commands that used it -- prodigal, metadata, rna_silva and rna_ltp -- spread their work with multiprocessing.Pool.imap_unordered instead. The class was a producer/consumer model written in 2014 around mp.Process, a queue of work and None sentinels, which is what imap_unordered has been since Python 3.2; keeping it cost 290 lines and three real faults. A producer that raised killed its worker without a word: the manager joined the dead process, put the sentinel, and the run reported success having processed fewer genomes than it was given, which for prodigal surfaced later as a KeyError against summary statistics that were never produced. None was both the sentinel and a legal return value, so the docstring had to instruct every caller never to return it, and a producer that grew an early return would have truncated a run at that genome. And the progress string was written to stdout with a carriage return while the loggers wrote to it too. The consumer still runs in the parent process, which is what the class was shaped to guarantee: the results of prodigal are collected in the loop that consumes them. The bar is tqdm, as everywhere else in the toolkit. run_seqs_file, the other half of the class, had no caller at all
- prodigal calls each genome's genes under the translation table it is given rather than under one it works out for itself. --trans_table takes the summary trans_table writes, and Prodigal is handed that table per genome, so it calls the genes once instead of calling them under tables 4 and 11 and keeping whichever coded more of the genome -- a rule that cannot express table 25 at all, and that had been deciding for every genome NCBI declared no table for. --tt_override corrects it: a TSV of genome_id and translation_table whose rows replace a prediction rather than being weighed against it, and which may name a genome the summary lacks, so a genome no classifier could answer for can still be called. Every genome of the release must have a table from one file or the other before any genes are called: a release the predictions do not cover is a mistake made upstream, and a run that met the gap genome by genome would meet it hours in, having already called the genes of everything ahead of it. Each genome's prodigal_translation_table.tsv records the table Prodigal reports having used and where that table came from, so a corrected genome says on disk that it was corrected; the coding densities are no longer written, only one table being run now and the other never measured
- prodigal_check is gone, and nothing in prodigal_manager.py reads a GFF or weighs a coding density any more. The command existed to report where the table Prodigal settled on disagreed with the one NCBI declares, and Prodigal no longer settles on anything: trans_table makes that comparison for the whole release in ncbi_tt_comparison.tsv, against a classifier rather than against a threshold on two numbers
- ncbi_tt_comparison.tsv carries a checkm_tt column: the table the coding density rule alone would choose, which is what Prodigal and CheckM do unaided (table 4 where it codes more than 5 percentage points more of the genome than table 11 and more than 70% of it, table 11 otherwise). It is computed from the densities gTranslate already reports, so nothing is called again, and it is what the three columns are for -- the rule cannot express table 25, so every genome gTranslate calls 25 is one the old way was never able to get right. The rule was written against fractions where gTranslate reports percentages, so the thresholds are scaled rather than the values
- trans_table runs the release in batches of --batch_size genomes (default 10,000), each with a directory of its own under --out_dir holding the batchfile it was cut from, the results, and files saying what has happened to it: RUNNING while a machine works on it, naming that machine, its PID and when it started, SUCCESS once its results are complete, FAILED where gTranslate returned non-zero. A release takes days to predict and nothing that takes days should have to be started again from the beginning: rerunning the command skips what succeeded and retries what failed, and several machines may be given the same --out_dir and will divide the release between them without being told which part to take, each claiming batches no other machine holds. The claim is made by linking a uniquely named file onto RUNNING rather than by creating it with O_EXCL, link() being the operation that is atomic across NFS, and a link reported to have failed is read back rather than believed, an NFS client being able to report a failure for a link it made. A claim this host left behind in a process that no longer exists -- which is what a machine reset leaves -- is reclaimed automatically; a claim from another host cannot be checked from here, so it is reported and left alone unless --reclaim says to take it, and a batch that FAILED is retried without a flag, the machine that failed it having cleared its own claim. SUCCESS is written before RUNNING is removed, the other order leaving a moment in which the batch looks neither claimed nor finished, which is the one state that would have a second machine repeat it
- trans_table leaves gTranslate's output to the terminal rather than reading it back a line at a time and logging it again. A progress bar redraws ONE line by returning the carriage to its start, and a carriage return is a line ending to whatever is reading, so every redraw arrived as a line of its own and a bar that should occupy one line occupied hundreds. Nothing is lost by not capturing it: gTranslate writes its own gtranslate.log into each batch directory, without the bars, which is the better record of a batch anyway, and the toolkit's own --log is left holding what the toolkit did rather than a transcript of what it ran. --silent discards the output instead of showing it. The two files this command reads are counted with a bar that erases itself when the file is read, a bar left behind on a finished read being a line in the middle of the log restating what the next line reports
- trans_table settles the batches before processing any of them, from the genomes sorted by accession, so which genomes are in batch N follows from the set of genomes and not from the order a genome_dirs file happens to be written in. The batchfiles are then authoritative: a later run reuses them and says so rather than partitioning the release again, since a second partition of a release that has since gained or lost a genome would move genomes between batches that are already finished. Each batch is processed from its own batchfile and not from the genome_dirs file, so the machine that runs a batch and the machine that planned it agree on which genomes it holds
- trans_table compares what gTranslate predicted against the table NCBI declares, writing ncbi_tt_comparison.tsv per batch and, once every batch has succeeded, for the release. A row per genome for which both are known: the accession, the two tables, agree or conflict, the coding densities under tables 4 and 11 that the prediction was made from, and the genome's NCBI lineage, read from the standardised taxonomy file --taxonomy_file now requires. What the comparison is for is the conflicts -- a genome whose genes GTDB would call under a table NCBI does not agree with -- and whether they fall together in the taxonomy, which is why the lineage is on the row rather than left to a join afterwards. A genome NCBI has not annotated declares no table and is left out rather than given a row saying nothing; those are counted and reported per batch. The summary columns are read by name, gTranslate having changed what it reports between releases, for the same reason the NCBI assembly summaries are never read by index
- the translation table NCBI declares for a genome is read by ncbi_utils.ncbi_translation_table(), moved there from prodigal_manager.py now that trans_table reads it too, with the GENOMIC_GFF_EXT naming the file it is read from. prodigal calls genes under that table and trans_table compares it against a prediction, so it belongs where what more than one command knows about NCBI's files already lives rather than in whichever command first needed it
- prodigal_check reports the genomes whose translation table Prodigal and NCBI disagree on, rather than always reporting none. It looked for <assembly>_genomic.gff where NCBI serves, and the mirror holds, <assembly>_genomic.gff.gz, so the guard that a genome has both files to compare was false for every genome and the table came out empty however many genomes disagreed -- which reads exactly like a release in which everything agrees. run_prodigal() has read the gzipped name all along; the two spellings are now one GENOMIC_GFF_EXT, since a name that two methods spell for themselves is a name that can drift apart again
- prodigal no longer stops with a KeyError on a genome whose GFF is empty. NCBI serves an empty GFF for a genome it has not annotated; the genome went into the list to call genes for but not into the dictionary of translation tables, and the wrapper looks that dictionary up for every genome it is handed. It is now recorded as None, the table being unknown rather than the genome unwanted, which is what the missing-GFF branch beside it already did and what makes Prodigal call the genes under tables 4 and 11 and keep the better
- prodigal writes each genome's prodigal_translation_table.tsv about THAT genome. The branch deciding whether to record 'used table specified by NCBI' or the coding densities tested a variable left over from an earlier loop, so every genome of a run was described by whichever accession that loop happened to end on: either all of them claimed NCBI named a table or none did, whatever was true of the genome being written. It now tests the genome being written, and tests whether NCBI actually named a table rather than whether the genome has an entry at all -- every genome has one, a genome NCBI declared nothing for having None, and for that genome Prodigal chose the table by coding density, so the densities are what there is to record
- trans_table predicts the translation table of every genome of a release with gTranslate, which puts a classifier trained on GTDB beside the two answers already available: the table Prodigal chose by coding density, and the table NCBI declares in the GFF it serves, which prodigal_check compares. gTranslate calls table 25 as well as 4 and 11, and answers for a genome NCBI has not annotated at all, which declares no table to compare against. It takes the same --gtdb_genome_path_file as prodigal, both commands asking what table a genome uses and both wanting every genome of the release; nothing is written into the genome directories, the predictions landing in --out_dir as one table for the release rather than a file per genome to be carried between releases. gTranslate is run as a subprocess and not imported: it requires Python >= 3.12 where this toolkit supports 3.8, and its predictions depend on pinned versions of scikit-learn, xgboost and lightgbm that importing it would make the toolkit's own dependencies. The genomes are handed over as a batchfile rather than by --genome_dir, which looks in one directory where a release is a tree, and matches on a file extension that _cds_from_genomic.fna.gz and _rna_from_genomic.fna.gz share with the genomic FASTA, so a directory sweep would hand over the genes of a genome as though they were the genome; the genome_dirs file already names every genome and where it is, so the batchfile is written from it and the run is exactly the release. The batchfile is written into --out_dir and kept, a prediction table being only as good as the list of genomes behind it, and it names each genome by the accession the genome_dirs file gives, so a prediction can be matched back to the directory it came from without canonicalising anything. A genome whose genomic FASTA is absent or empty is counted and named rather than left to fail in the middle of a run over half a million genomes, and a gtranslate that is not on the PATH is reported before the batchfile is written, with the module to load, since 'No such file or directory' does not say that the environment was not loaded. --cpus, --tmp_dir, --force, --keep_called_genes, --prefix and --custom_model_path are passed through to gTranslate, and an option the run was not given is left off the command line so that gTranslate's own defaults stay its own

0.1.8
- update_genomes --cpus defaults to 16 rather than to 1. Neither half of what the run does is bound by the CPU -- copying a genome is round trips to the file server, and a FASTA is read only where NCBI reissued one -- so one genome at a time was the slowest thing the command could be asked to do and the figure it was asked for by default. Against a mirror on NFS, 200 genomes of the size NCBI serves take 17.1s copied one at a time and 4.5s copied 16 at a time; a comparison in which every FASTA had been reissued takes 46.0s against 4.3s. 16 rather than the core count: past it the copying is bound by the link, and the hashing turns back down (64 was slower than 32). README.md carries the measurements
- update_genomes copies the genomes it adds --cpus at a time rather than one at a time. Copying a genome directory is a few dozen round trips to the file server and no arithmetic at all, so a copy spends its time waiting in a system call with the GIL released and threads are what overlap that waiting: 200 genome directories of the size NCBI serves (15 files, some 6 MB apiece) take 17.1s copied one at a time over NFS, 5.2s copied 8 at a time and 4.2s copied 32 at a time, the link itself being the ceiling from there. It went unnoticed while the genomes added were the few thousand new to a release, the comparison of the shared ones being the length of the run; with --fresh the whole release is added, and copying it one genome at a time is the whole of the run. Threads and not the processes the comparison uses, because the parent keeps the only handle on the reports and writes every row itself, so there is no listener process to arrange. The report row of a genome is still written before its copy is attempted, the report being the account of every genome the run handled, and its genome_dirs row still only once the copy has returned, so that file still names only genomes that are there. A copy that fails stops the run with what it raised, and the copies queued behind it are cancelled rather than run to completion -- a release quietly short of a genome is worse than one that did not finish being built. Only COPY_QUEUE_DEPTH copies per thread are queued at a time, the next genome being submitted as one finishes: submitting the lot up front is a line shorter and builds a future per genome before the first copy has finished, some 850 MB of them for a release of half a million genomes, which is the release --fresh is for
- update_genomes takes --fresh, which builds the release from the NCBI mirror alone: every genome the mirror holds is copied into the new release and reported as new, nothing is compared to the previous release, and no derived data is carried across, so the Prodigal results, marker hits, rRNA classifications and tRNA scans are all to be produced again. It is the run that starts a release from the NCBI data -- because there is no previous release to inherit from, or because everything derived from the genomes is to be regenerated whatever did not change -- and until now the only way to make one was to point --old_genome_dirs_file at an empty file, which said nothing about what was intended and reported every genome against a previous release that was not there. Nothing is recorded as removed, a run with no previous release behind it having nothing it could drop, and to_review.log is written and stays empty, nothing having been looked for in a previous release to be found missing. --old_genome_dirs_file is not read and is no longer required when --fresh is given, and a run without either is refused before the output directory is made rather than left to fail on open. What a run writes does not depend on how it decided what to write, so both ways of building a release open the same two reports and the same genome_dirs file through UpdateGenomes.release_reports()

0.1.7
- update_genomes no longer throws a genome's derived data away just because NCBI's published genomic FASTA MD5 changed. NCBI reissues a FASTA with rewritten deflines and untouched sequences -- a renamed organism, a corrected strain, a relabelled assembly -- and that MD5, being of the whole file, changes with them, so every release recomputed Prodigal, the markers, the rRNA classifications and the tRNA scans for thousands of genomes that had not changed at all. Where the two published MD5s disagree, sequences_md5() now hashes what the FASTA says the GENOME is: the contig IDs and the bases, ignoring everything after the first whitespace of a defline (NCBI's free text), the line wrapping and the base case (soft masking does not change which base is at a position). Equal sequences give a fourth outcome, 'genomic FASTA sequences unchanged', which carries the derived data across exactly as an unchanged file does. The contig ID and the boundary between one contig and the next are deliberately part of the digest and not ignored with the description: the derived data names the contigs it was called on and gives coordinates within them, so a genome whose contig was renamed, merged or reordered is regenerated however unchanged its bases. The check runs only where the MD5s already disagree -- a genome whose file is unchanged is never opened -- and hashes each file as it decompresses, writing nothing and holding no more than a line. The progress bar and the summary now carry four counts rather than three (unchanged, seqs unchanged, changed, failed), the per-database lines with them, and the two outcomes that keep derived data are reported apart rather than added together: how often the second is reached is the count of genomes NCBI reissued without changing, which is how much work a release would have repeated for nothing
- update_genomes writes the genome_dirs file of the release it builds, as genome_dirs.tsv in --new_directory: the headerless accession / absolute path / canonical accession TSV list_genomes writes and every later command reads. The run placed every directory, so walking the tree back with list_genomes afterwards only recovered what it already knew; list_genomes remains what indexes the MIRROR. Only genomes whose directory was actually WRITTEN are in it, so it can never name a path that is not there: a removed genome is not in the release, a genome that could not be compared was never copied (both MD5s are read before the copytree, so a failure leaves nothing behind), and a dry run, having copied nothing, writes no genome_dirs.tsv at all. Rows for compared genomes are written by the listener process along with their report rows, the one place a row cannot be torn in half by another worker writing at the same moment. The closing line gives the count read back from the file, with the usual per-database breakdown, so a release short of the genomes that went into it is noticed at the end of the run rather than by the step that reads it
- a GTDB release now holds RefSeq under refseq/ and GenBank under genbank/ instead of both under the mirror's own all/. NCBI's nesting within is kept exactly -- all/GCA/047/639/395/GCA_047639395.1_ASM4763939v1 becomes genbank/GCA/047/639/395/GCA_047639395.1_ASM4763939v1 -- so each database is a tree that can be indexed, moved or handed on by itself, which it could not be while the two were interleaved. release_genome_dir() takes the tail from the archive directory rather than dropping the first component, so --ftp_directory may name the mirror root or the all/ directory inside it and the same release path comes out either way, and it never rebuilds the nesting from the accession: NCBI defines that layout, ncbi_genome_sync laid it down from the URLs NCBI published, and a release that reshaped it would no longer be the thing --verify checks. A mirror path that does not run through the archive directory of the genome's own database is refused rather than placed somewhere unintended
- update_genomes updates RefSeq and GenBank in ONE run rather than two. It ran once per accession prefix because the GenBank run used to read the RefSeq run's output -- a GenBank assembly was dropped when its paired RefSeq assembly had been taken into the new release -- and that decision moved to select_genomes, which makes it against the RefSeq genomes it selects in the same pass. Nothing in update_genomes consults one genome while handling another, so the two runs bought only a second comparison pool idling until the first had finished; one run reads each genome_dirs file once and compares every shared genome in one pool of --cpus. The reports describe the release as one thing: report.log and to_review.log in place of report_gcf.log, gcf_to_review.log, report_gca.log and gca_to_review.log. What the split did report for free is kept -- RefSeq and GenBank differ by an order of magnitude in size and churn differently between releases, so a single figure can hide a database that changed wholesale or not at all -- and every count logged now carries the breakdown: the genomes read from each genome_dirs file, those to remove, add and compare, and the comparison summary, which gains a line per database (" - RefSeq: 9,655 unchanged, 812 changed, 0 not compared"). Both databases are always named, one with no genomes included, so the lines are seen to add to the total. A genome whose accession begins with neither prefix was silently dropped by both runs; it is now updated like any other and counted under 'unrecognised', which is named only when something falls under it
- update_genomes reports how the shared genomes came out, by outcome: "Compared 42,110 shared genomes: 39,802 with an unchanged genomic FASTA (derived data carried across), 2,308 changed (derived data left behind, to be regenerated)", with a warning naming the count of any that could not be compared at all. The report file always held a row per genome, but the numbers that say whether a release is a small update or a large one had to be counted out of it by hand. Counted by the listener process, which every row passes through already, so nothing is read back. --dry_run already made the comparison in full -- only the copying was ever withheld -- so a dry run now reports the same figures a real run would, before any file is copied, and says so. The 'Comparing shared genomes' progress bar carries the three counts as they accumulate (unchanged=9,600, changed=1,200, failed=1,200), in the order the summary names them rather than the alphabetical one set_postfix would impose on keyword arguments, so a comparison of a full release says how it is going while it runs and a run failing wholesale shows in the first seconds instead of at the end. The summary names EVERY outcome, the genomes that could not be compared included, so the three always add to the total; giving the total and then only the two FASTA outcomes read as a contradiction -- "Compared 16,843 shared genomes: 0 unchanged, 0 changed" -- when in truth every one of them had failed. A to_curate row now carries the exception MESSAGE and not only its type, since 'to_curate;ValueError' says almost nothing, and the run groups those failures by exception type in the log with one example each, so a systematic cause -- an --old_genome_dirs_file naming a tree that has since moved, say -- is named in one line instead of being found in a report with a row per genome. The 'to_curate' outcome is STATUS_TO_CURATE beside the other two rather than a literal; report_outcome() reads a row back, folding every to_curate reason into one outcome, curate_status() writes one, and tsv_safe() keeps a message with a newline in it from breaking the report
- ncbi_genome_sync --delete rebuilds a --retry file in one run: each listed genome's directory is removed immediately before that genome is fetched, by the same worker, so recovering from a <log>.bad no longer needs a --verify-only --delete pass to clear the directories and a second run to refetch them. Removing per genome rather than in an up-front pass means a stop leaves at most -j directories gone and not yet rebuilt, and those are the ones already recorded as "stopped:" in <log>.fail; a directory that will not delete is recorded as a failure and not synced, since fetching into it would rebuild the state being discarded
- ncbi_genome_sync no longer treats md5checksums.txt as the only authority. NCBI's md5checksums.txt and uncompressed_checksums.txt can contradict each other -- ~1000 genomes have a stale md5checksums.txt entry for <asm>_fcs_report.txt while the other table is right -- and believing the first alone meant those genomes could never sync at all (the bytes NCBI serves do not match the checksum NCBI publishes for them, so the download failed on every run) and that a verify of one mirrored by another route reported rot that was not there. Only UNCOMPRESSED files are eligible: that table lists the uncompressed form of everything, so a .gz appears in it under a name it does not have on disk (<asm>_genomic.fna against <asm>_genomic.fna.gz) and can never match -- asking about one is a request that cannot succeed, so it is refused before a request is made and a genome whose only failures are archives spends nothing. This also makes it structural rather than a matter of policy that the second table can never excuse a corrupt archive. A file md5checksums.txt rejects is now put to uncompressed_checksums.txt, in the sync and the verify alike, and passes if that table vouches for exactly those bytes under exactly that name; matching is by name, so the uncompressed digests that table also carries cannot excuse a corrupt archive, and a file neither table vouches for fails as before. The second table is a second opinion, never a first: it is fetched lazily, one request on the first disagreement in a genome and none for a genome that is clean, so a verify of a healthy mirror still makes no requests. It is fetched from NCBI rather than read from disk, since a local copy vouching for local bytes is two halves of the same claim, and is written into the genome directory when it settles something -- so some genome directories hold an uncompressed_checksums.txt and most do not, and its absence is never a fault. Each run's closing lines report how many files and genomes it settled. uncompressed_checksums.txt is read by read_uncompressed_checksums(), which is NOT the md5checksums.txt reader: the two files look nothing alike -- md5checksums.txt is `<md5>  ./<name>`, two spaces and no header, while uncompressed_checksums.txt is tab-separated with the name FIRST and the md5 second, under a `#file md5sum crc32 size` header -- and reading the second with the first's reader parses NOTHING out of it, which is indistinguishable from NCBI vouching for nothing, so every second opinion came back negative and the genomes the fallback exists for went on failing. Columns are found by name rather than position, since NCBI has grown its tables before; a copy with no usable header is refused rather than guessed at, and warns once per run
- <log>.bad gains a fifth column, failed_files: every file of that genome at fault, comma-separated and each tagged with which fault -- missing:NAME (the manifest lists it, the mirror does not have it), mismatch:NAME (present, bytes rotted) or unreadable:NAME (present, could not be hashed; the errno stays in the log). A row now says what to do about the genome, not only that something is wrong with it: a missing file any sync repairs, while a rotted one is trusted by the manifest fast path and needs --retry <log>.bad --delete. Verification no longer stops at the first failing file, so the column is every fault rather than the first found, and --delete removes the directory after the whole genome is checked rather than at the first failure, which would have taken the unchecked files with it. The four columns before it are unchanged, so a .bad still feeds straight back into --retry
- every file ncbi_genome_sync writes is now named after -l/--log rather than after the input table: -l r95/sync.log gives r95/sync.fail, .bad, .rm, .rm_dry_run and .extra, with .log stripped and any other extension kept (run.txt -> run.txt.fail). The log names the run, so one round's whole account of itself shares one stem; naming them after the input tied them to a table instead, so two rounds against the same selection overwrote each other while a round's .fail and .bad sat under unrelated names. Give each round its own --log. The rule that kept a retry from overwriting its own input (x.bad -> x.bad.fail) goes with it: run() already refuses (exit 2) a run whose .fail/.bad is its --retry input, and now says to give the round its own --log
- --delete is no longer rejected outside verification: it is legal with --retry (rebuild the listed genomes) and with --verify/--verify-only (remove what fails verification and what the selection does not list), and still refused on a bare --gtdb_selected_genomes sync, where it could only mean re-downloading the whole mirror

0.1.6
- refactoring release
- ncbi_ftp_manager.py and ncbi_ftp_manager_tools.py are split by command into ncbi_metadata_sync.py, select_genomes.py and update_genomes.py, and their classes renamed for the command they run: MetadataSyncManager is NCBIMetadataSync, SelectedGenomesManager is SelectGenomes, GenomeManager is UpdateGenomes (FTPTools keeps its name, alongside UpdateGenomes). Tests follow: tests/test_select_genomes.py, tests/test_update_genomes.py
- what more than one NCBI command knows about NCBI's files moves to ncbi_utils.py: REFSEQ_PREFIX and GENBANK_PREFIX, MD5_LINE_RE (from ncbi_genome_sync.py) and has_ftp_path. ncbi_genome_sync now decides a row has no usable ftp_path by calling has_ftp_path, the test select_genomes applies, rather than by its own copy of the expression
- every count ncbi_metadata_sync writes to the log separates thousands: node and name records, assemblies, the files of a taxdump, the files of a standardised taxonomy and the assembly summaries downloaded, so "Read 3,013,402 node records" rather than "Read 3013402 node records". Release numbers, taxids and date stamps are identifiers rather than counts and are still written as they stand
- the NCBI database table is defined once, in ncbi_utils.py: REFSEQ and GENBANK (directory name, label, accession prefix) as NCBI_DATABASES, with REFSEQ_PREFIX and GENBANK_PREFIX derived from it, and NCBI_HOST/NCBI_URL for the server every command fetches from. The naming of a saved assembly summary file is assembly_summary_filename() and assembly_summary_database(); ncbi_metadata_sync writes names through the one and select_genomes reads the database back through the other, in place of REFSEQ_SUFFIX and GENBANK_SUFFIX kept in step by hand
- the four columns every table handed to ncbi_genome_sync opens with are GENOME_COLUMNS in ncbi_utils.py, with table_header() building a header from them: the selection select_genomes writes and the .fail and .bad files the sync writes derive their headers from it, in place of a comment saying they must match. NCBI_NA names the null both read and write
- md5checksums.txt is read by ncbi_utils.read_md5_manifest(), which yields (md5, name) with the leading ./ removed, in place of the same parsing written out in the sync and in update_genomes; MD5_MANIFEST and GENOMIC_FASTA_EXT move beside it, and the sync names those files by them
- update_genomes copies a genome new to NCBI from the mirror whole, as it already did a genome held by both releases. The list of NCBI files to leave behind, kept for mirrors built before r237, is gone: the mirror is built by ncbi_genome_sync from its list of wanted files and holds nothing else, so the two copy paths now agree and there is one statement of which NCBI files GTDB keeps
- files are hashed by ncbi_utils.file_md5() and read a CHUNK (1 MiB) at a time, one helper and one block size in place of the sync's md5_file and CHUNK and ncbi_metadata_sync's file_checksum and DOWNLOAD_BLOCK
- ncbi_genome_sync takes -g as the short form of --gtdb_selected_genomes, as list_genomes already does for the same table
- every count ncbi_genome_sync writes to the log separates thousands -- genomes, rows, directories removed and added, requests, megabytes, throttled responses, breaker trips and seconds paused -- through format_count() and format_amount(), %-formatting having no comma flag. HTTP statuses, exit codes, PIDs, table line numbers and the settings echoed back from the command line (-j, rate=, max_age=) are identifiers rather than counts and keep their digits
0.1.5
- the NCBI taxonomy no longer carries the nomenclatural code NCBI appends to a name it holds under the SeqCode: "Patescibacteriaceae (SeqCode)" in names.dmp is read as Patescibacteriaceae, so f__Patescibacteriaceae matches every other spelling of the taxon. In a species name the brackets also failed the valid character check, which dropped the genome from the standardized taxonomy altogether
0.1.4
- ncbi_metadata_sync takes --group, PROK or FUNGI, and downloads the assembly summary files of that group alone: archaea and bacteria for PROK, the fungal summary of each database for FUNGI. Both groups get a standardised taxonomy, the fungal one generated with keep_subranks set, so it carries NCBI's subranks (13 ranks) where the prokaryotic one is the 7 GTDB curates
- the taxonomy files of a run are named for its group, ncbi_r<release>_prok_*.tsv and ncbi_r<release>_fungi_*.tsv, with the filter report as <group>_failed_filters.tsv, so both groups can be run into one release directory without either overwriting the other. A run reuses the taxdump already extracted under today's date rather than downloading it again, which also places the two groups against the one taxonomy
- NCBI_DOMAINS is replaced by NCBI_PROK_DOMAINS and NCBI_FUNGI_DOMAINS, with NCBI_GROUP_DOMAINS, GROUP_KEEP_SUBRANKS and GROUP_FILE_TAG saying what each group downloads, whether its taxonomy keeps subranks, and what its files are named for
- TaxonomyNCBI.parse_ncbi_taxonomy takes the assembly summaries as a list rather than as one argument per database and domain, a group holding four of them or two, and takes the file its filters report to
0.1.3
- ncbi_metadata_sync also downloads the fungal assembly summary of each database (assembly_summary_fungi_refseq.txt.gz, assembly_summary_fungi_genbank.txt.gz), so a release holds the fungal tables NCBI was serving when it was built. They are downloaded only: the 7 rank NCBI taxonomy is still built from the archaeal and bacterial summaries alone, and select_genomes is given whichever files it is pointed at
0.1.2
- lpsn pull_html writes the pages it passes over because their name duplicates a validly published one (LPSN lists such names quoted, with a -1 URL suffix) to <rank>_skipped.lst as name, URL, duplicate_name, and only when there is at least one; a skipped file left by an earlier run is removed otherwise. These were previously mixed into <rank>_failed.lst, which now holds download failures alone
0.1.1
- update_refseq and update_genbank are replaced by update_genomes, which updates both databases in one run from one mirror, one pair of genome_dirs files and into one release directory (--ftp_directory, --new_directory, --ftp_genome_dirs_file, --old_genome_dirs_file). RefSeqManager and GenBankManager are folded into GenomeManager, told apart by the accession prefix; the reports are report_gcf.log/gcf_to_review.log and report_gca.log/gca_to_review.log. The GenBank selection that lived in GenBankManager (gca_selection.log, --new_refseq_genome_dirs_file, --arc/--bac_assembly_summary) is gone: select_genomes makes that decision
- update_refseq and update_genbank now decide a genome held by both the previous release and the mirror from the MD5 of its genomic FASTA as recorded in the two md5checksums.txt files, in place of copying both directories to temporary space, decompressing and checksumming every file. The mirror's directory is taken whole, md5checksums.txt included; if the FASTA is unchanged the directories named by config.GTDB_DERIVED_DIRS_TO_COPY (prodigal, rna_silva_<version>, trna, rna_ltp_<version>) are copied across from the previous release, otherwise the derived data is left to be regenerated. The report now says "genomic FASTA file unchanged" or "genomic FASTA file changed" per genome, replacing the incomplete/modified/unmodified/new_metadata/new_hashes/old_folder_dir vocabulary; a derived directory the previous release lacks is noted in the review report. A dry run makes the comparison and writes the same reports as a real run, and copies nothing
- added SILVA_VERSION, LTP_VERSION and GTDB_DERIVED_DIRS_TO_COPY to config.py
- hmmsearch and top_hit --folder_suffix is now optional and defaults to config.MARKER_FOLDER_SUFFIX for the given --db (33.1_lite for Pfam, 15.0_lite for TIGRFAM), so the versions config.py declares are the ones a release is annotated into unless overridden. The derived marker names config.py carried for the old release update (PFAM_MARKER_DIR, *_EXT, *_SYMLINK_EXT, HMMER_EXTS_TO_GZIP) are removed with their last consumer
- update_genomes copies the mirror and the derived directories with symlinks preserved; following them copied each Pfam and TIGRFAM result file twice, once under pfam_*/tigrfam_* and once in place of the version-free symlink beside it
- update_refseq determines the genomes to remove, add and compare from the accessions in the FTP and previous release genome_dirs files alone, and no longer takes --arc_assembly_summary/--bac_assembly_summary: the mirror is built from the selection, so every RefSeq genome on it belongs in the release. Its report_gcf.log rows carry UNDEFINED in the domain column, the summary files having been the only source of a domain
- ncbi_genome_sync now takes --gtdb_selected_genomes, the table select_genomes writes, and treats it as the definition of the mirror: every genome directory under --root the table does not list is removed before anything is fetched, each recorded in <base>.rm before it is deleted. --retry syncs a .fail/.bad file without removing anything, and --dry-run reports how many directories would be removed, added and checked for updates, listing the removals in <base>.rm_dry_run without touching the mirror
- ncbi_genome_sync removes genomes in parallel instead of serially, and its climb up the emptied triplet directories asks rmdir rather than listing first: the pass is nothing but NFS round trips, and that is four fewer per genome as well as overlapping the rest. Measured on the production NFS server: 113 genomes/s serial, 465 at the default 8 threads
- ncbi_genome_sync takes --nfs-jobs (default 8), sizing the mirror walk and the removal; these touch the local mirror rather than NCBI, so --rate does not bound them
- removed clean_ftp; pruning the mirror is now the first step of ncbi_genome_sync, which holds the selection that says what the mirror should contain
- ncbi_genome_sync --verify/--verify-only, given the selection, now verify both halves of "the mirror equals the selection": every listed genome present and md5 clean, and nothing else present. Directories the selection does not list go to <base>.extra and fail the verification; --delete removes them
- ncbi_genome_sync interface: --root is now required with no default, --rate defaults to 20, and -l/--log is defined by the sync itself with .fail/.bad/.rm/.extra written beside it
- ncbi_genome_sync orchestration moved into an NCBIGenomeSync class with a run() method, so main.py drives it like every other manager; the thread workers and the state they share stay module-level
- list_genomes now takes -g/--gtdb_selected_genomes instead of -n/--new_list_genomes, and writes a genome_dirs file holding only the genomes of that release; its '-missing' and '-extra' reports are gone, that check being ncbi_genome_sync --verify. --genome_dir moved from -g to -d
- select_genomes ignores genomes NCBI serves no directory for (ftp_path na) in RefSeq and GenBank alike. Because RefSeq covers a genome only when the RefSeq assembly was itself selected, an unserved RefSeq genome no longer suppresses its GenBank counterpart: that copy is selected in its place, with the notes column naming the RefSeq assembly it stands in for and why. 639 RefSeq bacterial genomes are unserved on r237, and each would otherwise have left the release entirely
0.1.0
- moved to stream lined workflow where genomes for the next GTDB release are selected first before syncing with NCBI genome data
- added ncbi_metadata_sync, which populates the root NCBI directory of a release: the four per-domain assembly summary files (gzipped), and taxonomy/ holding the md5 verified NCBI taxonomy alongside standardised_taxonomy/ with the 7 rank taxonomy for the given release number
- assembly summary files may now be gzipped; every reader goes through ncbi_utils.open_summary, which takes either form
- removed parse_ncbi_taxonomy as a subcommand; the 7 rank taxonomy is generated by ncbi_metadata_sync
- the NCBI taxonomy parser no longer writes a line per assembly with an unresolvable taxid (2,521 of them on r237); it reports the count with three examples
- the NCBI taxonomy parser now reports through the 'timestamp' logger, so its output reaches the command's log file rather than only the console
- added select_genomes, which determines the genomes comprising a new release from the NCBI assembly summary files alone
- select_genomes now writes version_status and excluded_from_refseq, so its table is directly the input to ncbi_genome_sync; without version_status the sync fetches assembly_status.txt over HTTP for every genome
- fixed count_lines reading a gzipped file as text, which made select_genomes fail on the compressed summaries ncbi_metadata_sync writes
- ncbi_genome_sync now strips .gz when naming its .log/.fail/.bad outputs, so syncing from gtdb_selected_genomes.tsv.gz no longer yields gtdb_selected_genomes.tsv.gz.fail
- fixed ncbi_utils rejecting the '# assembly_accession' header NCBI wrote until 2020, which blocked ncbi_sync, update_refseq and update_genbank on archived summary files
- fixed parse_ncbi_taxonomy writing failed_filters.tsv to the working directory; it is now written beside the taxonomy it describes
- renamed ftp_manager.py to ncbi_ftp_manager.py and ftp_manager_tools.py to ncbi_ftp_manager_tools.py; GenericDatabaseManager is now GenericManager
- renamed the ncbi_sync command and ncbi_sync.py to ncbi_genome_sync; the mirror lock file keeps its .ncbi_sync.lock name so an upgrade cannot let two syncs share a root
